hey Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm and today we are talking  about frac poles or black poles so why  would you use a rack pole or a black  bulb in your training  first things first for a lot of tall  lifters they tend to have issues getting  down to the bar when it's on the floor  because keep in mind the height that a  deadlift starts off the ground is kind  of an arbitrary number just based on the  diameter of a 45-pound plate so relative  to body height it starts a little bit  higher above the ground for shorter  people and a little bit lower to the  ground for taller people so you know if  you're mid six feet tall then you doing  a deadlift for the 45 pound plate maybe  you know similar to someone who's 5 feet  tall doing a deadlift that's elevated 3  or 4 inches off the ground just relative  to body proportions so obviously if you  plan on competing in powerlifting you  need to get comfortable dead lifting  from the floor but especially for  learning the movements and even for your  whole training career if you don't have  competitive aspirations there's really  no reason for you to not do rack  deadlifts simply if that's a range of  motion that you're more comfortable with  so that's number one number two is for  learning the dead lifting movement a lot  of people will find that they can  control their lumbar spine in their  pelvic positions through a certain range  of hip flexion but then when they go  beyond that range their lower back tends  to round they tend to go into posterior  pelvic tilt which really isn't a good  idea especially under heavy load  so rack deadlifts can be very useful for  those people I'm starting at a rack  height that you can control your lumbar  position and then slowly working down as  you gain that motor control so that's  number two the third reason that you  might want to use them is simply to  overload the movement a lot of people  find that especially for high  apples not necessarily above-the-knee  but about the upper third of the shin  they tend to be able to pull more than  they can pull from the floor that can be  beneficial for people especially who the  deadlift tends to get in their head and  a particular number is intimidating them  if you can load that weight on the bar  and pull it even if it's through a  partial range of motion then when you  see that weight on the bar on the floor  it tends to be slightly less  intimidating and the last reason is to  increase training frequency a lot of  people especially those who aren't built  particularly well for deadlifts tend to  find that deadlifts from the floor are  quite a stressful movement and can you  know a hard deadlift workout can knock  you back three or four days can be  really difficult to recover from so by  either doing either a bulk of your  training for block pulls or alternating  pulls from the floor and block poles or  rack pulls you can train the movement  more frequently and also a hard deadlift  workout won't detract quite as much from  say squat workouts later in the week  where if you would have pulled from the  floor your low back may still be sore  and that could still be impacting other  lower body work later in your training  week the next thing we're going to talk  about our different styles of rack pulls  or block pulls the first type is pulls  from above the knee these tend to not  have hardly any correlation to what you  can pull from the floor they're very  good for building upper back extreme and  mass because you can typically pull a  lot more than you can from the ground  but in terms of actually strengthening  you through that entire range of motion  there tends to not be very much  carryover for example I've pulled 725  from the floor and my best high rack  pull starting from a couple inches above  my knee is 805 I believe 8:05 815 but I  know people with deadlifts in the mid  fives who can rack pull low eighths from  that same height the high rack Bulls do  tend to be a very good hypertrophy  exercise especially for your  absent upper back they make you look  sweet but they don't necessarily make  you that much stronger now don't let me  decide you from doing high rack polls  simply by saying it's not going to  improve your deadlift from the floor  very much because those big ol sweet  bang traps that's a very noble training  goal and it could still give you a  competitive advantage if it scares your  competition so those are black polls or  rap polls from above the knee the other  type obviously by process of elimination  are pulls from below the knee if you're  doing rack polls for transference to  powerlifting trying to improve your  deadlift from the floor that's where you  should be doing them from most of the  time a lot of people will actually find  that they can deadlift more from the  floor than they can from mid-shin  typically people can pull a little bit  more from just below the knee than they  can from the floor but then when you get  to 3 4 inches lower than that to where  the bars only elevated 2 to 4 inches off  the ground a lot of people can actually  pull more from the floor the reason for  that is when the bars starting all the  way on the ground you have more knee  flexion so you can get more assistance  from your quads in breaking the bar off  the ground and getting some speed going  so if you have a sticking point just  below your knee then getting that bar  speed going with the assistance of your  quads can help you get through that  sticking point whereas if you're  starting with the bar elevated slightly  off of the ground you have to start the  lift almost entirely with your posterior  chain and then you can't get quite as  much speed going hitting that sticking  point however especially if that applies  to you you'll probably find that rack  pulls from below the knee do improve  your deadlift max quite a bit simply  because that is a significant weakness  if you shorten the range of motion and  you're still weaker than you work  through the full range of motion you'll  probably find that doing those rack  pulls or black pools helps you build  more horsepower through that sticking  point so then when you have that added  strength and then throw in a little  extra bar speed from assistance for your  from your quads when you pull off the  ground again you'll probably find that  it helps her  and the last way to implement black  polls or rattles is Bob people's style  if you don't know who Bob people's is  he's a legend in the strength game he  was the first man to deadlift 700 pounds  and he did it at a body weight of only  about 170 whereas everyone else in the  race to 700 tended to be like 300-pound  monsters he had some very innovative  training techniques and the main one he  used to build his deadlift was  progressive range of motion training and  how he did that basically is he would  have a bar loaded and he would dig a  hole and he would stand in the hole and  deadlift the bar and then every day he  would put a little more dirt in the hole  and slowly fill it up so eventually he  was standing on the same level as the  bar again  and then he'd just add more weight on  the bar dig the hole again and repeat  the process this is something that it's  not quite as popular now as it used to  be but personally the biggest jump I've  seen in my deadlift in well since I got  my initial newbie gains in the deadlift  was actually you utilizing this style of  training I was doing sumo rack pulls I'm  a conventional deadlift er but for a  while I went over to the dark side  before I came back to the light  conventional poles are cooler keep that  in mind you may pull more sumo and a big  powerlifting total is also cool but just  in terms of what raises your  testosterone the most gets you the most  groupies it's definitely conventional so  just like advice anyway so I briefly  cashed in my man cart and started  pulling sumo and I was quite a bit  weaker off the ground then I was from  mid-shin there was a much bigger  difference there than there was for  conventional so I did a lot of sumo rack  pulls and I did them about people style  I started from slightly above the knee  and then every week I would lower the  bar by about an inch or so until I was  pulling from about 3 inches off the  ground and then  I would add more weight on the bar and  start back from slightly above my knee  and it actually didn't help my so  deadlift any at all  it put maybe 10 pounds on it but then on  a whim when I was starting to get  frustrated with my sumo I just went back  to conventional and randomly pulled a 30  pound PR my previous PR had been 645 and  I basically speed wrapped 675 which was  entirely unexpected and then two days  later when I was fresh I came in and  pulled 725 and so yeah that was like an  80 pound PR from not training  conventional at all and just doing this  progressive range of motion sumo rack  pulls certainly not saying that I can  guarantee an 80 pound PR for you but  just something to keep in the back of  your mind especially if your deadlift  training has been getting stale  progressive range of motion rack polls  can be a fun way to inject some life  into your deadlift training alright guys  that's it thanks for watching the video  if you liked it and you'd like to see  more please like and subscribe if you  have questions ask them in the comments  below if you'd like to see me make a  video about a particular topic leave  those suggestions in the comments as  well and if I like it I may make a video  about it and if not I won't but I still  love you alright guys peace
 

